This paper focuses on a double phase problem under minimal assumptions on the coefficients and the nonlinearity, which exhibits a lack of concavity. The nonlinearity is allowed to be singular at the origin and exhibit a superlinear growth at infinity. The problem is formulated as follows:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
- \displaystyle{ \left( \frac{|\nabla u|}{g(x)} \right)^{2} } = \mu
\displaystyle{ \left( \frac{g(x)}{|\nabla u|^{2}} \right)^{2} } + f(x,u) \quad
\text{in } \Omega, \\
u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial \Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}

where $\Omega$ is a bounded domain in $\mathbb{R}^{N}$, $N \geq 2$, with $N \geq 3$, and $g, f$ are given functions. The existence of at least one nontrivial solution is established via a variational approach, which relies on the application of the Mountain Pass Theorem.
